}; it will attribute each reaction before writing it - what does {{user}} mean by doing this and saying this - without this manual, it can only guess the most common and dramatic interpretations in the database. The manual is about the attribution step: each line is either a hard fact or a translation of a certain type of behavior of TA into its true meaning. You're not building a character, you're calibrating understanding. Complete the four processes in order. Your thoughts on steps one to three must be written in the <thinking></thinking> tag - write down your key judgments and basis for each step, and do not omit:\n1. Material collection. {{user}}'s speech in the conversation record (only look at TA's, don't look at AI's reply) is a first-hand behavioral sample: how long the sentence is, whether to use a commanding tone, whether to write actions, whether to hurt others, when to be silent - first record the observed patterns into thinking, and the subsequent translation blocks will all rely on them. Then align the description of <CharBrief> and the current Persona (it is the draft when \"perfecting the current character\", and the facts that still hold true must be kept in the final draft), the character card and the World Info (the relationship between TA and the opponent character, TA's position in this world, location, camp, and people's names will all use the original names in the World Info). In case of conflict, brief shall prevail; the meaning of the user's own words will always trump your observation and inference.\n2. Shaping piece by piece. Only do 【 the sections listed in the 】 checklist that need to be covered in this draft - don't even mention anything that's not on the list. The iron rule of the whole manuscript: this is a manual, not a literary work - each line is either a hard fact or a translation; just write what TA is, not what TA is not - the negative sentence will first put the negated word into AI's mind, and only use the \"no misreading\" line at the end of the block to intercept lines, and only block brief Or there are real misreadings in the dialogue records, just click on them; character tags are not included in the manuscript (tags are clues, not translations, and are only useful when it comes to behavior and meaning); compactness is a hard target - the manual is not the protagonist, the character card is. The longer it is written, the more it robs AI of the attention of the character. It is better not to write an uncertain translation, and a wrong translation will ruin the attribution than not writing it.\n【 output syntax 】 is said only once in the entire draft and takes effect everywhere: ①Quotation marks - \"\" and \"...\" are all counted as lines: only the skeleton is marked in the entire draft. Original words 〔...〕 The contents must be copied verbatim in the conversation record as TA actually typed them; any other lines must have zero quotation marks. If TA has not typed a word, it will not exist. ② Translation lines with \"meaning:\" - the left column is a description of the behavior (what TA does, how to type), and the right column only contains meaning (what does TA do, what does it mean): both cells are descriptive text, without lines or scenes. ③For frequency words, use words with room for most of the time/usually/occasionally. ④Keep the key names and ___ in all cases and replace them with real text, leaving no trace in the final draft; if the lines marked for deletion are deleted, the entire line will be deleted if there is no material, and no blanks will be left.\nThinking discipline: Only remember key judgments (one or two lines) in each piece of thinking. Fill in the blanks of the skeleton directly in the final draft, and do not pre-write the entire text in thinking. Each check section is done according to the corresponding ■ process block:\n■Basic settings: Only objective facts are registered in the entire block, and each key must be filled in. The more specific AI is to TA, the more accurate the attribution will be; each box is a line drawing, and no adjectives of perception (refreshing/exquisite/melancholy) are entered. brief For the keys not mentioned, choose a reasonable value that is consistent with the world view and known facts (the draft will be submitted to the user for review), and do not avoid it with \"unknown/to be determined\" (behavioral translation is not included in this list: if there is no material there, it will not be written). The relationship setting section is a must, don’t miss it. Fill in the blanks key by key:\n  {{user}} basic information:\n    Name: ___\n    Gender: ___\n    Age: ___\n    Identity: ___ (occupation/title/position in this world, use the original name in the World Info)\n    Appearance: ___ (one or two lines of white outline - hardware facts such as hair color, physique, and dressing habits, the characteristics you want AI to remember should be given priority)\n    Family circumstances: ___ (family members, financial status - one or two lines)\n    Residence: ___ (where and with whom)\n    Daily life: ___ (daily life trajectory - school/work/frequent places)\n    Key experiences: (Things that changed TA, one to three, one line per line, stop writing when the incident happened - do not use the \"From now on TA became...\" style summary)\n      - ___\n  Relationship settings:\n    Relationship with ___: (___ fills in the real name of the opponent character)\n      Starting point: ___ (how we met, a fact)\n      Current status: ___ (what is the relationship now)\n      Getting along: ___ (one thing TA often does to the other party + how the other party always responds, one sentence for each)\n■ Current Motivation：state what you 【infer】 {{user}} wants now from the supplied material. Knowing their aim prevents the AI from mistaking their methods for a different motive. For every line, first copy an original sentence, then state the aim. Evidence must be verbatim from the brief, transcript, or World Info; p