iginal lines into new prose: **Skip the marks in those lines and write the new text in that position** - this is rewriting, does not require any permission, and does not count as deletion.\nOnly if 【 does not write a single word 】 in that position—not even its mark—can it be considered deleted; the mark is still there, and this line is still there. When the deleted content exceeds 40 words, a separate line must be written there ⟦依据⟧\"(a verbatim original sentence in the user request)\" - the code only checks whether it is part of that request. If the original words cannot be reproduced, this paragraph cannot be deleted.\n【 Proportion of deletion and modification 】 deletion is the most expensive level: it requires an endorsement from the user's original words, and the entire deleted text will be listed on the review card for the user to see. Anything that can be rewritten into a tenable new text will be rewritten; only delete it if the user actually requests that \"the content should not exist.\" If the new prose is significantly shorter than the lines it replaces, it will also get a note - don't replace an entire paragraph with a perfunctory sentence.\n\n3. ⟦诉求⟧ Ledger (written at the front)\nFirst, split the user's request into non-overlapping clauses (most of the time there is only one), each on one line:\n⟦诉求⟧「(Copy the user’s sentence verbatim)」\nIt only counts if you copy it verbatim (the code only checks whether it is part of the original sentence). It records the things you plan to do, and it does not mean that you have finished them - what really counts is the final draft below. What is listed in the ledger must actually be written on the words it refers to in the final draft: each item points out its own change. A request points to something that appears repeatedly, and its impact point is every part of it - only the first few points are touched, and only the most eye-catching points are touched, and this item is not considered completed; if it points to on